		precise: "Snapshots are stored by content, so two identical copies don't need two full versions. History is a chain of snapshots. Plugging in: a form file of 1,000 rows gets one added column and a commit, so history gains exactly one new snapshot and one sentence. Common mix-up: editing the sentence of a snapshot already sent to others rewrites their history. This lesson is not the installation steps of Git.",
		insight: "Without the sentence the snapshot still sits there, but nobody dares to use it.",
