	"eda-miss": {
		title: "Missing values: a blank cell is not a zero",
		why: "So a customer who didn't state their age doesn't become a customer aged 0.",
		prior: "Duplicate rows are handled. But a blank cell is still not a number.",
		see: "This picture shows how to handle missing values. One side is a blank cell, the other is a cell filled with 0, and the two cases mean different things.",
		plain: "A blank means unknown, while 0 is a known number. Turning a blank into 0 is inventing data.",
		situation: "The age column of 1,000 customers has 100 blanks. The 900 who answered average 35. If you fill 0 for the other 100, the average drops to 31.5 and a model thinks there are customers aged 0.",
		use: [
			"Count the blanks per column before deciding to fill or delete.",
			"Fill 0 only when 0 is the truth, such as \"no spend\".",
			"When deleting rows, write the reason; when filling with the mean, remember it is a typical value, not that person's value."
		],
		precise: "If blanks occur completely at random, deleting rows only shrinks the sample. If blanks depend on another column, such as young people skipping the income box, filling must use those columns and is still an estimate. Plugging in: (35 × 900 + 0 × 100) / 1,000 = 31.5. Common mix-up: if blanks depend on the missing value itself, such as very high spenders not answering, filling the mean pulls the result toward a false safe side. There is no single right fill for every column.",
		insight: "Filling the table to make it complete does not mean you know the value.",
		symbols: []
	},
	"eda-out": {
		title: "Outliers: far away is not the same as wrong",
		why: "So the Tết week isn't deleted just because it doesn't look like ordinary weeks.",
		prior: "A blank must not become 0. A very large point must not be treated as junk either.",
		see: "This picture shows an outlier. One point stands alone, far from the rest; it may be a real holiday week or a typing mistake.",
		plain: "A far-away point is a warning flag, not a verdict. You need to check it before deciding whether to keep or drop it.",
		situation: "Most weeks have under 40 forms, but one week has 120. The IQR fence only flags that week. If it was Tết, 120 may be real. If someone typed an extra 0, 120 may be an error.",
		use: [
			"Use the fence to find unusual points, not to delete automatically.",
			"Ask whether the point matches real history.",
			"When you delete or cap a point, write a one-line reason, not \"because it's far\"."
		],
		precise: "The common fence is Q3 + 1.5 × IQR for the upper side and Q1 − 1.5 × IQR for the lower side, with IQR = Q3 − Q1. Plugging in: Q1 = 20, Q3 = 28 so IQR = 8 and the upper fence is 28 + 1.5 × 8 = 40. The week of 120 passes the fence. A point past the fence is only unusual relative to the middle of this sample; it does not prove the data wrong. Common mix-up: deleting a real point, such as a holiday week, makes the mean and the model lose exactly the part worth noticing. Capping a point (winsorizing) is also a decision, because you change a number that happened.",
		insight: "Deleting because it is far deletes what may be the whole story.",
		symbols: []
	},
	"eda-scale": {
		title: "Standardising with z: put columns on one ruler",
		why: "So the column in thousands of đồng doesn't swallow the column of visits just because its unit is bigger.",
		prior: "Far-away points were examined and not silently deleted. This step only changes the ruler.",
		see: "This picture shows standardising values. At first millions of đồng sit beside seconds, and afterwards both lie on the same ruler.",
		plain: "Standardising puts columns on one ruler. It does not create a new relationship between the columns.",
		situation: "Spend is in millions of đồng and watch time in seconds. If you compute distances to cluster, only the column with bigger numbers has a voice. With z-scores, every column carries equal weight.",
		use: [
			"Use it when comparing distances between rows or when penalising model coefficients.",
			"Subtract the mean and divide by the standard deviation of that same column.",
			"Don't expect z to create correlation, and don't expect it to turn a skewed column into a bell."
		],
		precise: "z = (x − x̄) / s. The sample mean of z is 0 and its sample standard deviation is 1. Plugging in: a customer spends 6 million đồng, the column mean is 5 million, the standard deviation is 2 million, so z = (6 − 5) / 2 = 0.5. Multiplying a column by a positive number does not change its Pearson correlation r with another column. Common mix-up: z does not make a skewed column symmetric. A decision tree doesn't need z to split, while distance and coefficient penalties do.",
		insight: "One ruler is so no column shouts over another, not to invent a straight line.",
		symbols: [
			"z is the distance from the value to the mean in standard deviations; in the example 0.5.",
			"x is the original value of a row; in the example 6 million đồng.",
			"x̄ is the column mean; in the example 5 million đồng.",
			"s is the column standard deviation; in the example 2 million đồng."
		]
	},
