		see: "This picture shows correlation versus causation. Forms, cream orders and the holiday season are all high, and the holiday season may be pulling both of the others.",
		plain: "Correlation is two columns moving together. Causation is when one column changes and the other changes while everything else stays fixed. A scatter plot cannot do the second.",
		situation: "In weeks when forms rise, cream orders near the shop also rise. Switching off the cream is not a media plan, since both may rise with the holiday season.",
		use: [
			"When two columns move together, list a third column that could push both.",
			"Don't recommend switching something off just because r is negative.",
			"To say \"this column pulls that one\" you need a comparison design, not another chart that moves the same way."
		],
		precise: "A third variable (a confounder) can create an r between two columns that do not pull each other. Plugging in: if the holiday season lifts forms by 30% and cream orders by 40%, the two columns can move together without either affecting the other. Common mix-up: time order is not enough either, because the order of events is not a knob you can turn. A negative r also does not mean \"harmful\". To speak about raising the budget, you need a comparison where the increase is not mixed with season and channel.",
		insight: "Moving together is a hint to ask a question, not a button to press.",
