	"py-str": {
		title: "Operations on a string return a new string; the old string doesn't change itself",
		why: "So one call of .strip() isn't taken as having cleaned the whole column.",
		prior: "Functions have return. A string is a data type, and operations on strings usually return a new copy.",
		see: "This picture shows a string. A str is an ordered sequence of characters. The slice [start:end] takes from the start position and excludes the end position. A method doesn't change the original string unless you keep the result: name.strip() without reassigning leaves the name with its spaces.",
		plain: "You need to reassign if you want to keep the cleaned copy.",
		situation: "\" tiktok \".strip() gives \"tiktok\", but the old variable doesn't change until you reassign. And \"tiktok\"[0:3] gives \"tik\".",
		use: [
			"To keep the clean copy, reassign: name = name.strip().",
			"When comparing text, unify upper and lower case first if the problem doesn't distinguish them.",
			"Don't add a number to a string; convert the type on purpose."
		],
		precise: "Indexes start at 0. Negative indexes count from the end, -1 being the last character. len gives the number of characters, not the number of words. An f-string is just a way to insert values into a new string; it doesn't compute a wrong ratio for you. Plugging in: \"tiktok\"[0:3] is \"tik\" and \"tiktok\"[-1] is \"k\".",
		insight: "Calling strip without keeping the result has fixed nothing.",
		symbols: []
	},
	"py-list": {
		title: "A list is ordered, editable, and indexed from 0",
		why: "So the first element isn't wrongly called element number 1.",
		prior: "A string is a sequence that can't be edited in place. A list is an editable sequence.",
		see: "This picture shows a list. A list keeps order and lets each slot change. Index 0 is the first element, -1 the last. The slice [a:b] makes a new list, excluding b. form[1] on [80, 90, 70] is 90, not 80.",
		plain: "Count from 0. Slicing makes a new copy, not a \"look for me\".",
		situation: "With weeks = [80, 90, 70], weeks[0] is 80 and weeks.append(60) adds 60 at the end. A list of channel names has no meaning for taking an average.",
		use: [
			"The first slot is 0 when giving an index to someone else.",
			"When you need a copy that leaves the original untouched, slice or copy.",
			"A nested list is a list of lists, not a table with column names."
		],
		precise: "append adds one element. extend joins each element of a collection. Appending a whole list gives one element that is that list. The assignment new = old makes no copy; both names point to one list, so editing through one name is seen through the other. Plugging in: weeks = [80, 90, 70] then weeks[1:3] is [90, 70].",
		insight: "Two names can be one list.",
		symbols: []
	},
	"py-dict": {
		title: "Dictionary: a missing key raises an error, only get tolerates absence",
		why: "So a new channel doesn't crash the whole loop just because of an unknown key.",
		prior: "A list gets values by position. A dict gets values by key.",
		see: "This picture shows a dictionary. A dict pairs each key with a value, each key appearing once. form[\"tiktok\"] with no such key raises KeyError. form.get(\"tiktok\") returns None, or a default you provide. A new channel appearing midway is normal.",
		plain: "Use get when a missing key is a possibility. Use square brackets when a missing key is an incident.",
		situation: "With {\"gdn\": 80, \"tiktok\": 40}, fetching \"fb\" with brackets stops the program. get(\"fb\", 0) returns 0, and you must understand that 0 here means \"not seen\", not \"measured as 0\".",
		use: [
			"Keys should be things that stay still, such as codes or normalized names.",
			"A missing key should be handled as a branch, not left as a crash.",
			"A list can't be used as a key."
		],
		precise: "A key must be an immutable type. Strings, numbers and tuples of immutable things work; lists don't. Assigning to an existing key overwrites the old value, not adding a second row. Iterating a dict iterates keys, in insertion order in current Python versions, but you shouldn't rely on that order as a ranking.",
		insight: "A 0 returned by get is not yet a measurement.",
		symbols: []
	},
	"py-tup": {
		title: "A tuple is ordered but doesn't allow editing slot by slot",
		why: "So a row of data that has been fixed isn't overwritten slot by slot by a later row.",
		prior: "A dict needs an immutable key. A tuple is an immutable sequence, so it can be a key while a list cannot.",
		see: "This picture shows a tuple. A tuple resembles a list in being ordered and indexed, but differs in not supporting append and not allowing reassignment of a single slot. (80, \"tiktok\") is a closed row. To change the form count, make a new tuple; don't edit the old one.",
		plain: "Use a tuple for a row of data you don't want edited in place.",
		situation: "With week = (80, 2000), the command week[0] = 90 raises an error. That is the tuple's lock, not a broken machine.",
		use: [
			"A fixed row should use a tuple.",
			"To add elements, use a list or build a new tuple.",
			"Unpacking several names at once runs only when the number of names matches the number of elements."
		],
		precise: "The comma makes the tuple, not the parentheses. (80) is just the number 80 in parentheses, while (80,) is a one-element tuple. Unpacking a, b = (80, 2000) needs exactly two names. A tuple containing a list can still have that list edited: the shell is fixed, the inside may change. Plugging in: a, b = (80, 2000) gives a = 80 and b = 2000.",
		insight: "Not being able to assign a slot is a feature, not a shortcoming.",
		symbols: []
	},
	"py-set": {
		title: "A set removes duplicates and promises no order",
		why: "So a list of \"unique customers\" isn't read as a sorted table.",
		prior: "A list keeps duplicates and keeps order. A set keeps each value once and promises no order that you can rank by.",
		see: "This picture shows a set, a collection. Adding an element already present doesn't lengthen the set. Testing \"is in the set\" is very fast. A set has no reliable slot 0: three rows with the same customer code put into a set leave one code, and you no longer know whether that code was first or last in the file.",
		plain: "Use a set to ask \"who is there\". Don't use a set to keep order by week.",
		situation: "{\"a\", \"b\", \"a\"} keeps only two codes. The order printed is not the order you put in.",
		use: [
			"Count unique customers by putting codes into a set and taking its length.",
			"When you need order, keep a list or sort explicitly.",
			"Set elements must be immutable, like the keys of a dict."
		],
		precise: "The operator | is union, & is intersection, - is difference, and they return a new set. A set is not a table: it has no columns, and the duplicate rows are gone so you can't trace back. Wanting both uniqueness and a representative row is no longer a set but a way of choosing rows, and you must write down the choice. Plugging in: 5 rows containing 3 distinct customer codes give a set of length 3.",
		insight: "Losing duplicates also loses the order you were remembering.",
		symbols: []
	},
