	"py-np": {
		title: "A NumPy array holds one data type, arranged as a rectangle",
		why: "So a channel name slipping into a numeric array doesn't turn the whole column to text.",
		prior: "A list tolerates mixed types; a numeric array doesn't. That is the price of fast column-wise calculation.",
		see: "This picture shows a NumPy array (ndarray). An array has a shape, usually (rows, columns), and every cell has the same dtype. A mean along an axis is an operation on numbers. Putting a string into a numeric array either raises an error or turns the whole array to text, and .mean() is no longer the mean of forms.",
		plain: "Put only numbers into a numeric array. Keep names elsewhere.",
		situation: "Three weeks [80, 90, 70] have mean 80. The shape (3,) is a sequence of length 3, not a table with 3 columns. Indexes still start from 0.",
		use: [
			"Check dtype and shape before taking the mean.",
			"axis=0 goes down the rows, axis=1 goes across the columns; say which.",
			"Channel codes should not share an array with forms."
		],
		precise: "NumPy forces every cell to one type. An object array means you have lost the advantage of a numeric array. Operations on the whole array aren't a for loop, and one NaN cell can turn the whole mean into NaN unless you choose to skip it. Shapes (n, 1) and (n,) don't always share an operation. Plugging in: (80 + 90 + 70) / 3 = 80.",
		insight: "One string in a numeric column changes the whole column, not just one dirty cell.",
		symbols: []
	},
	"py-ser": {
		title: "A Series adds by label (index), not by the position the eye sees",
		why: "So two \"same length\" columns aren't added with customers misaligned.",
		prior: "A numeric array adds by position. A Series is a column with labels, and the labels are what get aligned.",
		see: "This picture shows a pandas Series. Each row of a Series has an index. Adding two Series pairs them by index: matching labels add, non-matching labels give missing values rather than wrongly adding the row below. The weekly series of GDN and TikTok are both length 4, but the date labels are off by a day, so adding by position adds the wrong dates.",
		plain: "Check the index before adding two columns.",
		situation: "A Series for days 1 to 4 plus a Series for days 2 to 5. By eye you see four numbers stacked on four numbers. A Series adds only the days that coincide, days 2, 3 and 4, and the mismatched days stay empty.",
		use: [
			"Set the index to what identifies a row, usually a date or a code.",
			"To add by position, reset the index explicitly and accept the risk.",
			"NaN after label alignment means no match, not 0."
		],
		precise: "A Series carries one dtype for its values, like an array. The index can have duplicates. With duplicates, alignment multiplies rows and is no longer \"one row per day\". iloc takes by position, loc takes by label. Mixing them up fetches the place the eye sees with the wrong date, or the reverse. Plugging in: days 1 to 4 plus days 2 to 5 give 3 days with values and 2 days NaN.",
		insight: "The same length is not the same days.",
		symbols: []
	},
	"py-frame": {
		title: "DataFrame: loc uses labels, iloc uses positions, and a loc slice includes the end",
		why: "So a slice \"up to day 4\" doesn't lose day 4 or take an extra row.",
		prior: "A Series is a column. A DataFrame is several columns sharing one index, that is a table.",
		see: "This picture shows a DataFrame. loc[row, column] uses labels, iloc uses positions starting at 0. loc[\"a\":\"c\"] includes c, while iloc[0:3] excludes slot 3. Slicing labels from day 1 to day 4 with iloc by feel easily loses a day or takes an extra one.",
		plain: "State whether you are holding labels or positions.",
		situation: "For a 5-row table, iloc[0:2] is the first two rows. But loc from label 0 to 2, when the labels are 0, 1, 2, includes the row labeled 2, so you get three rows, not two.",
		use: [
			"Filtering by a condition on a column should state the condition, not slice arbitrarily by row number.",
			"Text columns and numeric columns should be handled separately, not taking mean of the whole table.",
			"Split the training and test sets before any row duplication."
		],
		precise: "A row is a unit you must be able to name. df[\"form\"] is a Series, while df[[\"form\"]] is a one-column table, and the two don't share every function. loc with a label slice includes both ends, unlike list slicing and unlike iloc. Assigning to a slice sometimes touches the original and sometimes doesn't, depending on whether the slice is a view. To be sure, assign a column with a Series aligned on the index, then reread a few rows.",
		insight: "Including the end or not makes two different slices.",
		symbols: []
	},
	"py-plot": {
		title: "A chart is an answer, and the axis must have a unit",
		why: "So a column of counts isn't read as a rate.",
		prior: "The table already has a unit per row. A chart must not change the question relative to that unit.",
		see: "This picture shows choosing a chart. Bars compare groups, lines follow time, the axis states the unit, and the title is a statement of a finding, not the name of the chart type. 200 forms and 20 forms are magnitudes. 4% and 8% are rates. Drawing 200 beside 20 and calling a channel better has not yet divided by clicks.",
		plain: "Choose the chart by the question, state the unit, and don't let the axis lie by itself.",
		situation: "GDN has 80 forms on 4,000 clicks, that is 2%. TikTok has 40 forms on 1,000 clicks, that is 4%. A bar chart by form count says GDN is bigger, and a chart by rate says the reverse. Both are right, for two different questions.",
		use: [
			"Write the question before calling the plot function.",
			"State the unit on the axis.",
			"Don't use one line to join channels that have no time order."
		],
		precise: "A Matplotlib or Seaborn figure draws only what you give it; it does not divide by the denominator for you. A truncated axis not starting at 0 makes a small difference look like a jump. Two units on one axis is a misleading figure unless you label both scales clearly. Color is not an analysis. Plugging in: 80 / 4,000 = 2% and 40 / 1,000 = 4%.",
		insight: "With no denominator, don't draw a race.",
		symbols: []
	},
