	"sql-like": {
		title: "LIKE is a simple pattern; regex is a different tool",
		why: "So a pattern \"contains the word form\" isn't written as a regular expression that each system understands differently.",
		prior: "You have counted and grouped. Filtering text is another kind of WHERE, and easy to confuse with regex.",
		see: "This picture shows LIKE and regex. LIKE uses % for any run of characters and _ for exactly one character. REGEXP or RLIKE is a regular expression, system-dependent, and does not follow the same rules as LIKE. WHERE channel LIKE '%tok' matches tiktok, but also any string ending in tok.",
		plain: "Use LIKE when searching for a containing phrase. Use regex when you need an exact character rule, and name the system.",
		situation: "A code looks like F- followed by two digits. LIKE 'F-__' matches F, a hyphen and any two characters, which is not yet \"exactly two digits\". To check exactly two digits you need the regex of the system actually in use.",
		use: [
			"Start with a phrase: 'tok%', end with: '%tok', contain: '%tok%'.",
			"When you need exact digits or a character rule, use the regex of the exact system and name it.",
			"Handle date columns with date functions, not regex."
		],
		precise: "An unanchored pattern can match in the middle of a cell. Upper and lower case depend on the column's collation. A function or regex wrapped around a column usually prevents the index on that column from being used. LIKE is not a safe way to split a date stored as text. Convert it to a date type. Plugging in: LIKE '%tok' matches 'tiktok' and also 'xtok'.",
		insight: "'%tok' does not mean \"exactly the tok channel\".",
		symbols: []
	},
	"sql-date": {
		title: "A date is a date type; a string that looks like a date can't do calendar operations",
		why: "So \"yesterday\" isn't a string cut by hand.",
		prior: "LIKE is about text. A date is a type of its own, and wrapping a function around a date column can defeat the index.",
		see: "This picture shows how to treat dates. Store dates with the date type. YEAR, DATE and DATEDIFF work by the calendar, while cutting a string works by text and breaks when the format changes. A missing day in the table means there is no such row, not that the row has 0 forms, unless you created a 0 row.",
		plain: "Don't call YEAR() on a column if you need that column's index to filter a range.",
		situation: "Filter November with date >= '2026-11-01' and date < '2026-12-01'. This half-open range doesn't depend on the number of days in the month. Wrapping MONTH(date) = 11 may not use the index.",
		use: [
			"Keep the DATE or TIMESTAMP type, not text.",
			"Filter by a range, and avoid wrapping a function around the column when possible.",
			"A date with no row is not counted as 0."
		],
		precise: "A time zone can shift a TIMESTAMP to another day when you look only at the date part, so state the column's time zone. DATEDIFF counts by the system's rule, usually the number of day boundaries, not hours divided by 24. The string '11/02/2026' is unclear between 2 November and 11 February. Plugging in: dates from '2026-11-01' up to before '2026-12-01' are exactly the 30 days of November.",
		insight: "Cutting date strings is an error waiting for the format to change.",
		symbols: []
	},
	"sql-nest": {
		title: "A subquery is not \"more advanced\" than a join",
		why: "So a correlated query isn't kept just because it looks hard.",
		prior: "You have filtered dates and text. A nested query is a way to write \"among the rows that satisfy another query\".",
		see: "This picture shows a subquery. An uncorrelated subquery runs independently. A correlated subquery reruns for each row of the outer query because it uses that row's column. \"Customers whose forms are above the average of their own channel\" is a correlated query. It is not automatically faster than a JOIN with an averages table.",
		plain: "Choose the readable query, measure the time if slow, and don't upgrade by nesting more.",
		situation: "IN (SELECT code FROM ...) gives a set of codes. A JOIN with that table is usually the same idea and shows the row unit more clearly. If the set contains NULL, IN and NOT IN differ, because NOT IN meeting a NULL may return no rows.",
		use: [
			"A subquery that uses no outer column runs once.",
			"A subquery that uses an outer column should be labeled a correlated query.",
			"When NULL is in IN, don't assume NOT IN is still the complement."
		],
		precise: "EXISTS asks whether any row exists, not for values, and is often better than IN when you don't need the column. A correct correlated query can still scan the whole inner table for every outer row. A JOIN with a grouped table is a way to see whether you are multiplying rows. Plugging in: 1,000 outer rows and 500 inner rows can mean up to 500,000 comparisons. \"Hard to read\" is not an optimization.",
		insight: "Nesting more doesn't make the query more correct.",
		symbols: []
	},
