	"sql-nwin": {
		title: "Naming a window (WINDOW) is only naming; the meaning is unchanged",
		why: "So a WINDOW written once isn't read as a new kind of calculation.",
		prior: "LAG and frame already have meaning. Naming only avoids copying the same OVER.",
		see: "This picture shows a named window. WINDOW w AS (PARTITION BY ... ORDER BY ...) and then OVER w reuses exactly that specification. Two functions that share one window name share the same grouping and the same frame. No function is \"preferred\" over another.",
		plain: "Use a name when a long OVER is repeated. A name does not change what the calculation means.",
		situation: "A running total and LAG both use PARTITION BY channel ORDER BY date. Name the window once for both. Changing the frame in one function doesn't change the other, unless it uses that very name.",
		use: [
			"When a long OVER must be repeated, give it a name.",
			"Check which functions are using that name.",
			"A name can't fix a week missing from the frame."
		],
		precise: "The WINDOW clause is an alias for a window specification. The system doesn't turn it into an intermediate table you can see. Renaming doesn't change the result. Two different names are two different specifications, even if they look the same to the eye. The default frame remains each function's default if you don't override it.",
		insight: "A window name is not a new calculation.",
		symbols: []
	},
	"sql-proc": {
		title: "A stored procedure is a saved script that can act outside the query",
		why: "So a \"run procedure\" button isn't mistaken for a transparent SELECT.",
		prior: "CASE keeps a rule in the query. A procedure keeps a whole script in the system, and a reader of the SELECT doesn't see all of it.",
		see: "This picture shows a stored procedure and a function. A procedure is a saved program that can contain several statements and can write to tables. A user-defined function (UDF) returns a value and shouldn't hide a table scan. A function wrapped around a column in WHERE may run row by row and bypass the index.",
		plain: "If you only need a readable query, use a CTE; there's no need to promote it to a procedure.",
		situation: "A \"close the week\" procedure both SELECTs and INSERTs into the closed table, so calling it again writes more. It is not a view. A tax UDF that scans a price table inside has a cost that doesn't show on the outer query.",
		use: [
			"For read-only naming, use a CTE or a view.",
			"If it writes to tables, call it a procedure and say where it writes.",
			"For a function placed in WHERE, ask whether it wraps the column and scans a table."
		],
		precise: "Execute permission on a procedure doesn't let the caller see all the tables inside. That may be what you want, and it is also what makes some errors hard to find. A function should be pure: same input gives same output and writes no data. A function that writes tables is a procedure wearing a function's name. This year's tax rule shouldn't sit in a procedure with no owner.",
		insight: "Hiding the script doesn't make the number more correct.",
		symbols: []
	},
	"sql-cur": {
		title: "A cursor goes row by row; one query handles the whole set",
		why: "So a \"to be safe\" loop doesn't replace a statement that could be expressed as a set operation.",
		prior: "A procedure can hide several statements. A cursor is the slowest kind: take a row, do something, move on to the next row.",
		see: "This picture shows a cursor. A cursor opens a result and walks each row in the program. The set-based approach is one statement for the whole set of rows. Summing forms by channel with a FETCH loop is rewriting GROUP BY, slower, and easy to forget CLOSE.",
		plain: "Prefer to write one statement. Use a cursor only when the next step is an outside action needing row-by-row handling, and you accept the slowness.",
		situation: "Sending an email to each eligible customer may need going row by row, because an email is an outside action. The total of forms doesn't need that: it is just a GROUP BY statement.",
		use: [
			"Summing, counting and filtering should use one statement.",
			"Consider a cursor only when there is an outside action per row.",
			"Open means close; a cursor forgotten open is a held session."
		],
		precise: "A cursor holds state: open, fetch a row, end of data, close. Forgetting to close on a web session is a resource leak. A set-based statement lets the system choose how to read, while a cursor forbids that. It is not more accurate than GROUP BY; it is a programming loop sitting on top of SQL. Plugging in: summing forms over 1,000 rows with one statement is one scan, with a cursor it is 1,000 fetches.",
		insight: "Going row by row doesn't make addition more correct.",
		symbols: []
	},
