	"ens-oob": {
		title: "Out-of-bag rows score that tree; they are not next week",
		why: "So an out-of-bag score isn't reported as a result on a week that hasn't happened.",
		prior: "Each tree has a bag of data and leaves out some rows. The left-out rows can score that very tree, provided they are truly different customers.",
		see: "This picture shows out-of-bag (OOB) data. OOB rows are those not drawn into one tree's bag, so they can score that tree. But they still belong to the training set, not a future month. If rare people are duplicated before splitting, one copy sits in the bag while the other is called \"out of bag\", and the score looks good only because it is the same person.",
		plain: "Use OOB only after the final test set has been set aside, and don't call it future data.",
		situation: "The training set has 1,000 customers. Each tree leaves out about a third of the rows, and those score that tree. Next week isn't among these 1,000 customers. Duplicating 8 churned customers before splitting is how OOB gets to lie.",
		use: [
			"Set aside the final test set before training.",
			"Understand OOB as the training rows not in that tree's bag.",
			"Don't duplicate rows before drawing the bag."
		],
		precise: "Each tree has a different OOB set. The average of the scores on those sets is an estimate on the training set, usually more optimistic than a set held out by time, because OOB rows are still from the same period as the bag. Plugging in: the probability a row is not drawn into a bag is about (1 − 1/n)^n ≈ 0.37, so about 37% of rows are OOB for one tree. OOB does not replace a check by time. Shared people between bag and OOB break the estimate in the manner of data leakage.",
		insight: "Out of bag is not next week.",
		symbols: []
	},
	"imb-smote": {
		title: "SMOTE creates a point in between, not a customer",
		why: "So a synthetic point doesn't slip into the test set.",
		prior: "Duplicating a rare row creates no new customer. SMOTE doesn't either: it only places a point on the segment joining two rare rows.",
		see: "This picture shows SMOTE. The algorithm interpolates between minority-class rows in the space of numeric columns, and the new point is not a person who bought. If SMOTE is done before splitting, a point in between can carry part of the training set and part of the test set, which is data leakage.",
		plain: "Split first, apply SMOTE only on the training set, and don't score on fake points.",
		situation: "Two customers churned, one spending 2 million and one 4 million. SMOTE may add a point at 3 million, but no customer spent exactly 3 million. If that point lands in the test set, the model is scored on a person who does not exist.",
		use: [
			"Split training and test sets first.",
			"Interpolate only on the training set.",
			"Keep the test set as real rows."
		],
		precise: "Interpolation assumes the line segment between two rare rows still belongs to the rare class. For a text column, a line segment has no meaning. Plugging in: between 2 and 4 million, a point with random factor 0.5 sits at 2 + 0.5 × (4 − 2) = 3 million. Doing SMOTE on the whole file and then splitting is leakage, the same family as duplicating before splitting. SMOTE doesn't fix a wrong label: it multiplies the labels already there, dirty ones included.",
		insight: "A point in between is not a third customer.",
		symbols: []
	},
