	"xa-text": {
		title: "Text functions return text; they don't turn text into numbers",
		why: "So the LEFT of a code isn't added up like money.",
		prior: "Cells in C0 already have types. The text functions of advanced Excel still return text.",
		see: "This picture shows text functions such as LEFT, RIGHT, MID and TRIM. All of them return text. To get a number you need a deliberate type conversion on top. LEFT of \"80 form\" takes out \"80\", which is still text, so SUM skips that column and gives 0, while & joins strings; to make it a number, convert on purpose (VALUE or *1).",
		plain: "Cleaning text is one step. Converting to a number is a later step, and only when it really is a number.",
		situation: "For code F-12, LEFT takes the letter F and TRIM removes extra spaces. None of them gives the number 12 unless you split and convert the type. A column of codes shouldn't be added.",
		use: [
			"Use TRIM when there are extra spaces.",
			"Split the code and the numeric part into two columns.",
			"Don't use SUM on a text column."
		],
		precise: "CONCAT joins text and doesn't add money. The VALUE function or multiplying by 1 can turn a digit string into a number, and will error on text that isn't numeric. That is what you want: the error surfaces instead of a silent, short total. Plugging in: VALUE(MID(\"F-12\", 3, 2)) gives 12. PROPER and UPPER don't turn a code into a correct lookup key if the other table doesn't follow the same rule.",
		insight: "Cutting up a code does not yield an amount of money.",
		symbols: []
	},
	"xa-count": {
		title: "COUNT counts numeric cells; COUNTA counts non-empty cells",
		why: "So a column of names isn't counted with COUNT and then reported as having no customers.",
		prior: "Text functions don't give numbers. Counting functions also look at the type of the cell, not at whether a cell \"looks like it has data\".",
		see: "This picture shows the counting functions. COUNT counts numeric cells. COUNTA counts non-empty cells, text included. COUNTBLANK counts empty cells. A column of 100 customer names where COUNT gives 0 doesn't mean no customers; it means the wrong function was used.",
		plain: "Count names with COUNTA, count measurements with COUNT. State what you are counting.",
		situation: "There are 100 names, and the form column has 90 numeric cells and 10 empty cells. COUNT of the form column is 90, COUNTA of the name column is 100. Reporting \"100 forms\" is wrong.",
		use: [
			"To count people in a text column, use COUNTA, after removing the header row.",
			"To count measured numbers, use COUNT.",
			"An empty cell is a case of its own; don't let COUNT turn it into \"no people\"."
		],
		precise: "COUNT skips text, empty cells and errors, and gives no warning that it skipped. A column entirely of text codes gives COUNT = 0 though the column is full. COUNTA also counts a cell holding only a space if it isn't truly empty, depending on the content. Plugging in: 100 names give COUNTA = 100, 90 form numbers give COUNT = 90 and COUNTBLANK = 10. Look at a few real cells; don't trust only the count.",
		insight: "COUNT of 0 on a column of names is counting with the wrong function.",
		symbols: []
	},
	"xa-match": {
		title: "INDEX and MATCH can look left; VLOOKUP cannot",
		why: "So you don't have to cut up the whole table just because the column to fetch is left of the key column.",
		prior: "C0 already has VLOOKUP, #N/A and the approximate-match trap. This session is how to look up when the direction isn't to the right.",
		see: "This picture shows INDEX combined with MATCH. MATCH returns the position of the key. INDEX takes the cell at that position in the column you name, and that column can be on the left. Channel name is in column A, code in column B: VLOOKUP by code can't fetch the name because it sits on the left, while INDEX/MATCH can.",
		plain: "When the key is not the leftmost column of the range to fetch, don't use VLOOKUP.",
		situation: "MATCH with match_type 0 is exact search. If not found it gives #N/A, the same meaning as VLOOKUP with FALSE. Don't leave match_type as 1 if the column isn't sorted.",
		use: [
			"Use MATCH with 0 when you need the exact key.",
			"Use INDEX on the column holding the answer; it doesn't need to sit on the right.",
			"#N/A still means not found; don't wrap it into a fake name."
		],
		precise: "INDEX(return_column, MATCH(key, key_column, 0)). The argument 0 is exact search. The argument 1 is approximate search on an ascending column, with the same trap as VLOOKUP TRUE. MATCH doesn't care which side the return column is on. Plugging in: the key \"K3\" sits at position 3 of the key column, so INDEX takes the 3rd cell of the return column. Inserting a row above the range shifts positions if the range isn't locked with $, as learned in C0.",
		insight: "Looking left is not a trick; it is just the direction of the column.",
		symbols: [
			"c is the column holding the answer to fetch; in the example the channel-name column.",
			"k is the key to look up; in the example the code \"K3\".",
			"d is the column of keys that MATCH searches; in the example the code column.",
			"0 is exact-match mode, not approximate."
		]
	},
	"xa-what": {
		title: "Changing an input cell does not mean seeing the future",
		why: "So a what-if table isn't taken out as a verified forecast.",
		prior: "Lookup fetches a number already in the table. What-if asks \"if this cell were different, what does the formula give\".",
		see: "This picture shows what-if analysis. Data tables and scenarios change the input cells of a formula you wrote. They don't learn from new data. Changing the price from 100 to 120 changes the profit in the file by the formula, but next week's price may not be 120.",
		plain: "Call this a test on the formula; don't call it a forecast.",
		situation: "The formula is profit = units sold × price − cost. A two-way table varies units and price. Each cell is one recalculation of that formula, not a week that has happened.",
		use: [
			"Write the formula and mark the cells that are inputs.",
			"Read the table as scenarios of the formula.",
			"Talking about next week is forecasting, in C4, not a data table."
		],
		precise: "Goal Seek reverses one input so an output hits a number. It is still your formula, and several inputs can reach the same target. Scenario Manager is just saved sets of inputs. Plugging in: units 50, price 120, cost 3,000 give profit 50 × 120 − 3,000 = 3,000. No set is the truth until the event happens and you measure again.",
		insight: "An assumption cell is not an observation.",
		symbols: []
	},
	"xa-macro": {
		title: "A macro repeats clicks; it doesn't make decisions",
		why: "So a macro button isn't handed the job of \"choose the channel for me\".",
		prior: "What-if is only a formula. A macro records click steps, including wrong clicks.",
		see: "This picture shows a macro. A macro replays a recorded sequence of actions. If the file's structure changes, that sequence clicks the wrong place. A macro recorded while the wrong channel was filtered will filter the wrong channel next time, very consistently.",
		plain: "Use macros for dull repetition. Decisions live outside the macro.",
		situation: "Pasting numbers into a fixed area each morning is something a macro can do. The matter of \"should the budget go up\" isn't in a macro, since no click contains that question.",
		use: [
			"Record only steps that were right on a sample file.",
			"When columns change, fix the macro and don't hope it understands.",
			"Don't put a business decision in a click."
		],
		precise: "A macro is code, usually VBA, even if you record it with buttons. It can edit data without asking again. A file with macros is a file with a program, so opening one from a stranger runs their program. A macro adds no test, no model and doesn't know the row unit of the table. Plugging in: a macro pasting 30 rows into A2:A31 overwrites those 30 rows every time it runs.",
		insight: "Consistent does not mean correct; a macro is only consistent.",
		symbols: []
	},
