	"tree-gini": {
		title: "Gini: a good question makes both sides cleaner",
		why: "So the tree doesn't split on a question that leaves both sides as mixed as before.",
		prior: "You know a question sends customers left or right. This lesson scores whether the two sides become cleaner.",
		see: "This picture shows Gini impurity. The starting node is mixed, with an impurity of 0.48. After the cut, each side is cleaner.",
		plain: "Gini measures how mixed a node is. A useful cut makes that impurity fall.",
		situation: "A node has 100 customers, of whom 40 stay and 60 leave. You try a question that splits this node into two nodes and want to know whether it makes both sides cleaner.",
		use: [
			"Compute the impurity of the left node and the right node.",
			"Multiply each side's impurity by the share of people going that way, then add them.",
			"Keep the question that lowers the weighted impurity the most."
		],
		precise: "Gini G = 1 − Σ pₖ², where pₖ is the share of label k in the node. Plugging in: the root node has G = 1 − (0.4² + 0.6²) = 0.48. A cut gives a left node of 40 people (30 stay, 10 leave): G = 1 − (0.75² + 0.25²) = 0.375. The right node has 60 people (10 stay, 50 leave): G ≈ 0.278. The weighted impurity is 0.4 × 0.375 + 0.6 × 0.278 ≈ 0.317, lower than 0.48, so the cut helps. A node of a single label has G = 0. Entropy uses logarithms for the same purpose.",
		insight: "The tree keeps the question that makes both sides cleaner, and that is its whole search.",
		symbols: ["G is the Gini impurity of a node, 0 when the node is pure; in the example the root node is 0.48.", "pₖ is the share of label k in the node, in the example 0.4 stay and 0.6 leave."]
	},
